    "agent_last_message": "The tool is finished and builds cleanly with `cargo build --release --manifest-path output/Cargo.toml`. All 29 of its own tests pass, and its output matched an independent Python implementation of the spec exactly on about 30,000 random files and on large files of 50\u2013210 MB.\n\n**Choices where the spec was ambiguous:**\n- **Malformed input means invalid UTF-8.** A file that isn't valid UTF-8 (e.g. Latin-1) exits with status 1, gives the byte offset of the bad sequence, and writes no output. If you'd rather count such files instead of rejecting them, it's a small change.\n- **Lowercasing covers all of Unicode**, not just A\u2013Z, so `CAF\u00c9` and `caf\u00e9` count as one word. The spec says reported words are \"in lowercase\", which ASCII-only lowercasing wouldn't guarantee.\n- **Exactly the six listed characters separate words.** Other Unicode spaces, such as the no-break space, count as part of a word.\n- **Ties are ordered by Unicode code point.**\n- **Exit codes beyond the spec:** an output path that can't be created exits with 1. A write that fails partway exits with 2, as do panics and out-of-memory (both tested).\n- **Non-ASCII characters in the JSON are written as `\\u` escapes**, so the report parses the same in any reader.\n\n**Speed:** files of 8 MiB or more are split across up to 8 threads, and memory use depends on the number of distinct words, not the file size. Two of my own bugs came up in testing and are fixed. One was a hang when a large file had invalid bytes in many places. The other made multi-threaded runs slower than single-threaded ones on files with millions of distinct words. On this machine, which is limited to 4 CPUs:\n\n| File | 1 core | 4 cores |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 MB English-like text | 0.83 s | 0.34 s |\n| 100 MB of mostly unique tokens | 1.2 s | 0.96 s |\n\n**Older Rust:** it also builds and passes its tests on Rust 1.70. I ship `Cargo.lock` in its older format, because older cargo versions can't read the one newer cargo generates.\n\nTo check builds on older Rust ",
